partners are: node 327 early, node 658 mid-window, and node 1838 late all contact the same fixed ~30-node partner pool. The group members merely rotate which one carries the traffic, so no old contact set gives way to a new one. High per-node activity (90-95 events, 30-35 partners) and the absence of any burst (z>=1.5) rule out volume and popularity explanations."}, "raw_final_response": "## Analysis\n\n**Primary test (the specified metric):** Scanning 40 candidate change points with a 5000-unit half-window on each side, the peak dissimilarity (1 \u2212 Jaccard) of the group's contact sets is **0.2683 at t=33052** \u2014 well below the 0.6 threshold. The series is flat and low throughout (0.12\u20130.27), the signature of gradual background turnover, not a regime switch. The before/after contact lists at the peak differ by only a handful of transient members (358, 585, 1145, 1705 leaving; 93, 186, 305, 652, 681, 730, 1361 arriving) against a shared core of ~30 partners.\n\n**Confounder controls:**\n- *Volume / popularity:* Group members are highly active (90\u201395 events, 30\u201335 partners each; step_03), and the window holds 5,444 events. High activity alone does not create a switch.\n- *Burst artefact:* No activity bursts at any z\u22651.5 (step_07); the mild dip in bins 40052\u201346051 (step_04) is a rate change, not a partner change.\n- *Time-ignored edge churn \u2014 the key trap:* Edge-level diffs look spectacular (Jaccard **0.0**, 61 removed / 65 added at 43052; 82 removed / 71 added end-to-end; steps 05, 06). But inspecting the *contact identities* shows this is an illusion: node 327 in [28052,33052] and node 1838 in [53052,58052] have **byte-identical** 30-node neighbourhoods (steps 08, 09), and node 658 mid-window has the same 30 plus one extra (step_10). The churn comes from *which group member* takes a turn contacting a **fixed external partner pool** \u2014 a rotation of internal endpoints, not an old set of contacts giving way to a new one.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_d9748f02\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [327, 390, 411, 464, 556, 658, 674, 876, 1220, 1418, 1676, 1838],\n  \"window\": [28052, 58052],\n  \"confidence\": 0.9,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"Peak before/after contact dissimilarity with a 5000-unit half-window is only 0.2683 (at t=33052), far below the 0.6 criterion, and the turnover series stays in the 0.12-0.27 band across all 40 candidate change points - gradual background churn, not an abrupt transition. Edge-level snapshot diffs appear to show a total rewiring (Jaccard 0.0, 61-82 edges removed and added), but this is an artefact of ignoring who the external partners are: node 327 early, node 658 mid-window, and node 1838 late all contact the same fixed ~30-node partner pool. The group members merely rotate which one carries the traffic, so no old contact set gives way to a new one. High per-node activity (90-95 events, 30-35 partners) and the absence of any burst (z>=1.5) rule out volume and popularity explanations.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 51413, "total_output_tokens": 3889, "total_tokens": 55302, "total_latency_ms": 60365, "llm_calls": 7, "schema_repairs": 0, "tool_protocol_errors": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}